div.p:
Research on marital status clarified the ways that women navigated their workspaces. Waitresses, chambermaids and union organizers used variations of their legal names, mixing middle names, nicknames, maiden names, divorced or common-law partner surnames, and even aliases. Honorifics, too, were often revised as a form of economic or even physical protection;

 Several high-profile cases of waitresses being murdered by people they had met in their workplace occurred during the case study’s years of enquiry.

for example, “Mrs.” was used when single or living common-law to secure employment, housing, or simply for reasons of propriety, or “Miss” when in fact married, in order to secure employment. In choosing how they represented marital status when working in exploitative and sometimes sexualized workplaces and professions, women gained some power over their privacy and their choice of workplace and enhanced their physical security. Researchers found that the HREU included couples who shared leadership, as well as couples who were active within different unions. Item sets such as union minutes and personal photographs suggest that unionism provided culture, friendship, and social and political identity, crossing over into personal life. The research team confirmed previous findings of Kaminski and Pauly (2013) and Kaminski and Yakura (2008) that indicate that women who ascended to union leadership were more likely to remain unmarried or experience marital disruption, compared to male counterparts. Those who were married faced burdens as caregivers and their potential careers as leaders were impacted by these responsibilities. Hinshaw (2014) argued that women union leaders were significantly less likely to have children and were more often divorced or never married than male leaders.
